{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And Joseph took both of them.</b> Joseph brought them near to his father as was proper and in the correct order, for he brought Manasseh the firstborn to his father's right and brought Ephraim to his left." "<b>And behold:</b> Jacob knew concerning Joseph that he would bring them near before him thus, and therefore he sent his right hand upon the head of Ephraim the younger and his left upon the head of Manasseh, because he knew by the holy spirit that the merit of Ephraim was greater than that of Manasseh; and this is what he too said: ״he also shall become a people, and so on״ — ״but his younger brother shall be greater than he.״ And because of this it says ״and he crossed (וישכל) his hands״; Rabbi Hananel explained: he crossed his hands one over the other, end of quote. And his intent is to say that he did not exchange the boys and did not move them from their place, but rather he exchanged his hands and crossed them one over the other, placing his right on the head of the younger and his left on the head of Manasseh who was the firstborn. But this is not correct, for what need is there to cross the hands one over the other, when Scripture says that he did not change the boys — only his hands?" "<b>Rather:</b> the correct interpretation of ״he crossed (וישכל) his hands״ is — from the hands of Joseph. For Jacob saw that Joseph had placed Manasseh at his father's right and Ephraim at his left; ״he crossed his hands״ — meaning he outwitted Joseph's hands and exchanged the boys. And the meaning of ״he crossed his hands״ is clarified by ״Israel sent (וישלח) his right hand,״ and Scripture says: ״Israel sent out his right hand and laid it on the head of Ephraim״ — that he exchanged Ephraim and drew him to his right, ״and his left on the head of Manasseh״ — that he exchanged Manasseh and drew him to his left. He did not exchange his hands or cross them at all, but rather he exchanged the boys. And this is the meaning of ״he crossed his hands״ — that is, he outwitted the hands of Joseph, for Joseph had not brought them near to him in such a way but only Manasseh at Israel's right and Ephraim at his left. And the blessing took effect upon them through this laying on of hands, as is written concerning Joshua (Numbers 27:23): ״and he laid his hands upon him,״ in order to bestow the holy spirit upon the one upon whom hands are laid. And from this matter was the ordination (semikhah) in the time of the Sages, and likewise the priestly blessing with raising of the hands — for it is not possible for a priest raising his hands to lay his hands upon each and every one of Israel; therefore he would spread his palms upward in order to draw down the power of blessing from the source of blessing above. And this is ״he crossed (וישכל) his hands״ — from the root שכל, understanding and wisdom, for he directed his ten fingers to draw the blessing from the source of wisdom and understanding. And so the Targum says: אחכמינון לידוהי — he guided his hands with wisdom, in the manner of (Psalms 134:2): ״lift up your hands [in] holiness and bless HaShem.״ ״Holiness״ is the wisdom, which is the source of blessing. And understand this." "<b>For Manasseh was the firstborn.</b> Although Manasseh was the firstborn — because he knew the merit of the younger over him. And this is like the language of (Psalms 41): ״heal my soul, for I have sinned against You״ — although I have sinned against You. And likewise, ״forgive us, our Father, for we have sinned.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 48, :notes {2 "Source citation reads (במדבר כ״ז:כ״ג) but labels it ביהושע; the verse cited (ויסמך את ידיו עליו) is Numbers 27:23. Citation label retained as written; verse reference normalized.", 3 "Source writes (תהילים מא), missing the verse number; the quotation רפאה נפשי כי חטאתי לך is Psalms 41:5. Reference left as written by the author."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:20.666709230Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}